<Layout title="Sources & Methodology | CyberIntel" description="How CyberIntel collects, validates and presents cybersecurity research data.">
<div class="shell"><section class="hero"><span class="tag">TRANSPARENCY / RESEARCH STANDARDS</span><h1>Sources & <span class="accent">methodology.</span></h1><p>Know where the intelligence comes from, when it was collected, and what it can—and cannot—tell you.</p></section>
<section class="section"><div class="cards"><article class="card"><span class="eyebrow">01 / VULNERABILITIES</span><h3>CISA KEV</h3><p>Reports vulnerabilities with evidence of exploitation. Being listed does not establish whether a specific organisation is affected or compromised.</p><a href="https://www.cisa.gov/known-exploited-vulnerabilities-catalog" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View official source ↗</a></article><article class="card"><span class="eyebrow">02 / EXPOSURE</span><h3>Shodan</h3><p>Aggregated search observations indicate indexed service visibility. Counts are not unique-host or confirmed-vulnerability counts and may change with coverage.</p><a href="https://www.shodan.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">View official source ↗</a></article><article class="card"><span class="eyebrow">03 / REPORTING</span><h3>Publisher feeds</h3><p>Headline metadata, publisher attribution and source links are collected periodically. Stories are not verified independently unless clearly marked as original research.</p><a href="/world/">View current feeds →</a></article></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="panel standards"><h2>Editorial and technical standards</h2><ul><li><strong>Source-first:</strong> every news item links to its original publisher.</li><li><strong>Freshness:</strong> displayed collection timestamps refer to the most recent successful ingestion, not event occurrence.</li><li><strong>Failure transparency:</strong> unavailable or stale feeds should be clearly distinguishable from current records.</li><li><strong>Scope:</strong> offensive security material relates to systems owned or explicitly authorised for testing.</li><li><strong>Risk:</strong> CVSS, KEV status, EPSS and Shodan exposure are different signals; none alone proves exploitability.</li><li><strong>Data minimisation:</strong> public exposure summaries should not include private credentials or sensitive assessment results.</li></ul><p class="muted">CyberIntel provides research and educational information, not a guaranteed complete or real-time threat feed. Security decisions should be confirmed with vendor and primary-source advisories.</p></div></section></div>
<style>.standards{padding:28px 32px}.standards h2{margin-top:0}.standards ul{padding-left:22px;line-height:1.9;color:#bdd0dc}.standards li{margin-bottom:12px}.standards strong{color:#f0f7fa}</style>
</Layout>
